{
  "id": "lhkd5dx",
  "title": "Chapter 10: Acids, Bases and Salts",
  "order": 59,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "16mxqh9",
      "content": "Acids, bases and salts are important classes of chemical compounds that play a crucial role in chemistry and daily life. These substances participate in numerous chemical reactions and are widely used in industries, laboratories and households."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "pH Range",
          "value": "0 - 14",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Neutral Point",
          "value": "pH 7",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Acidic Range",
          "value": "< 7",
          "icon": "clock"
        },
        {
          "label": "Basic Range",
          "value": "> 7",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "0yqwqwq"
    },
    {
      "id": "25tna59",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Acids"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Acids produce hydrogen ions (H+) in aqueous solution, taste sour, and turn blue litmus red.",
        "Bases produce hydroxide ions (OH-) in solution, feel slippery, taste bitter, and turn red litmus blue.",
        "Alkalis are bases that dissolve completely in water, such as NaOH and KOH.",
        "The pH scale quantifies the concentration of hydrogen ions to indicate acidity or alkalinity.",
        "A neutralization reaction between an acid and a base yields salt and water."
      ],
      "id": "45fn2ap"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bu60myx",
      "content": "• Acids are substances that produce hydrogen ions (H⁺) when dissolved in water."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wjho70b",
      "content": "• They have a sour taste."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fizzvmx",
      "content": "• They turn blue litmus paper red."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "498cp9i",
      "content": "• They react with metals to produce hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8u9k2uv",
      "content": "Examples of common acids:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "dcaorfv",
      "content": "• Hydrochloric acid (HCl)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "qyu6h5w",
      "content": "• Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "d0wqrlb",
      "content": "• Nitric acid (HNO₃)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "h4vofjf",
      "content": "• Acetic acid (CH₃COOH)"
    },
    {
      "id": "b9kmq9a",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Bases"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5kyykr4",
      "content": "• Bases are substances that produce hydroxide ions (OH⁻) in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ecador7",
      "content": "• They taste bitter and feel slippery."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jclfsjb",
      "content": "• They turn red litmus paper blue."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1sm17y4",
      "content": "Examples of bases:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "509ef8b",
      "content": "• Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ic0w41i",
      "content": "• Potassium hydroxide (KOH)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "fct1m2y",
      "content": "• Calcium hydroxide Ca(OH)₂"
    },
    {
      "id": "jb7ihhu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Alkalis"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7czupqa",
      "content": "Alkalis are bases that dissolve in water and produce hydroxide ions. All alkalis are bases but all bases are not alkalis."
    },
    {
      "id": "wes0dtr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Properties of Acids and Bases"
    },
    {
      "id": "nisb2a2",
